{"agent_id":"pp3g-P7oT-tight-scope-code-review-issue-153-your-only-task-024a","agent_seq":2,"timestamp":"2026-08-03T21:16:33.428733127Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"a18609af-a463-4254-bdde-1c908ac4b608","comment_uuid":"6c49754e-a34d-41de-b9ca-12f29458a52e","author":"pp3g-P7oT-tight-scope-code-review-issue-153-your-only-task-024a","content":"## VERDICT: #145 wrapSSE patch — edge cases, fragility, bugs, regression risk\n\nScope: reviewed ONLY the #145 patch (remove the `text/event-stream` content-type guard in `wrapSSE`) against the #144 root-cause analysis and the #145 verification table. No other code/investigation per task scope.\n\n**Summary**: The patch is directionally correct and aligns with upstream PR #39516. The controlled test proves it converts the application/json infinite-hang into an abort-within-chunkTimeout. But it is NECESSARY, NOT SUFFICIENT: it does not touch the silent infinite retry loop, it adds a false-positive abort risk for slow-but-legitimate non-SSE bodies, and as applied it is an ephemeral binary byte-patch that will not survive an opencode upgrade.\n\n### Findings (severity per finding)\n\n**F1 — Silent infinite retry loop is NOT fixed (Severity: MEDIUM, pre-existing, unchanged by patch)**\nThe patch makes `chunkTimeout` fire for application/json, so the json hang now enters the SAME silent retry loop SSE already had (#144 §3 row 1: \"silent infinite retry loop, process alive → killed only by outer timeout\"). The #145 verification table confirms it: \"retries with exponential backoff ... process alive+retrying until outer 40s kill (EXIT=124)\", with \"Zero ERROR entries\". The agent loop still sees zero errors and the process still claims RUNNING. The patch trades a permanent dead hang for a retrying-but-eventually-killed hang — strictly better for recoverability, but the root cause (no visible error, no clean termination) is untouched. The #135 watcher kill-and-restart remains the only real recovery. Net on this axis: neutral-to-positive, not a regression, but do NOT mistake this patch for a fix of the silent loop.\n\n**F2 — False-positive abort risk for legitimately-slow non-SSE bodies (Severity: LOW–MEDIUM, NEW regression risk introduced by patch)**\nThe original guard deliberately EXCLUDED non-SSE responses so slow-but-legitimate non-streaming bodies would not be aborted by a per-chunk deadline. Removing the guard now applies `chunkTimeout` (300s in prod) to ALL bodies. Any non-SSE response with a gap > chunkTimeout between chunks (e.g., a slow non-streaming completion, a large/slow download through the provider) will now be ABORTED where before it would have completed. With 300s this is unlikely for normal API traffic, but it is a behavioral regression vs. the original design intent and should be acknowledged. The #145 healthy-json control passing does NOT exercise a slow-but-legitimate non-SSE body, so this risk is untested by the verification table.\n\n**F3 — Fix is an ephemeral binary byte-patch, not a durable source rebuild (Severity: MEDIUM–HIGH, fragility/regression risk)**\nThe live fix is a same-length in-place byte replacement (`if(!$.headers.get(\"content-type\")?.includes(\"text/event-stream\"))return $;` → `if(false)return $;/*…*/`) in the 179MB compiled Bun standalone. This is not a source change. Any `opencode upgrade` / npm update will silently overwrite `opencode.exe` and RE-INTRODUCE the bug with zero warning. The #145 result acknowledges this (\"NO rebuild needed for the fix\" but lists rebuild steps separately). Until the canonical one-line `provider.ts` patch is applied in a fork build and the binary is pinned, the protection is temporary. Recommendation: prioritize the source rebuild + fork pin so the fix survives upgrades.\n\n**F4 — Abort-controller blast radius must be confirmed per-request (Severity: MEDIUM, verify)**\n`wrapSSE` calls `ctl.abort()` on timeout. The patch expands the surface on which `ctl.abort()` can fire (now any slow non-SSE body). If `ctl` is a single controller shared across requests/session rather than created fresh per request in `resolveSDK`, a false-positive abort (F2) could kill unrelated in-flight requests. The patch does not change the abort wiring, but it widens when it triggers. This needs verification against `resolveSDK` before declaring the patch safe. If `ctl` is per-request, severity drops to LOW.\n\n**F5 — Content-type ambiguity / expanded wrap surface (Severity: LOW)**\n- Responses with NO content-type header: previously excluded (`null?.includes()` → unwrapped, no timeout); now wrapped and timeout-protected. Arguably an improvement (catches header-less hangs) but a behavior change.\n- ALL other content-types (`application/octet-stream`, multipart, etc.) are now wrapped. Generally fine for hang detection, but expands the wrapped surface uniformly. No bug, just note the broader coverage.\n\n### (1) Does removing the guard break fast-completing legitimate non-SSE responses? — NO (low risk)\nThe per-chunk timeout only fires on a gap > chunkTimeout between chunks. A fast, complete non-SSE response has no such gap, so the timeout never fires. The #145 verification table confirms: \"healthy application/json response: normal completion, EXIT 0, no retries\" and \"healthy text/event-stream stream: EXIT 0, no retries\". The wrapping mechanism is the same proven path already used for SSE. So fast non-SSE responses are not broken. The only residual risk is F2 (slow-but-legitimate non-SSE), which concerns SLOW responses, not fast ones.\n\n### (2) Missing/ambiguous content-type behavior? — See F5 (LOW)\nNo missing handling; the removal is unconditional. The only ambiguity is the no-header case (now protected) and the broadened surface. Acceptable, but document it.\n\n### (3) Does it worsen the silent infinite retry loop? — NO (neutral-to-positive)\nIt does not worsen it; it makes json reach the same loop SSE already had, which is recoverable by the outer timeout (vs. permanent hang before). The loop itself is unchanged and remains a separate problem (#135 watcher).\n\n### (4) Is there a safer alternative? — YES\n- **A) Keep the patch but surface a visible error when chunkTimeout fires** (currently zero ERROR entries). Making the abort produce a retryable error the agent loop can see/escalate would address the silent-loop root cause, not just the hang. Highest-value addition.\n- **B) Apply a SEPARATE, longer body-completion deadline to non-SSE bodies** instead of reusing the same `chunkTimeout`, preserving the original intent of not aborting slow-but-legitimate non-streaming responses while still catching true hangs (the #144 \"add a general body-read deadline\" recommendation).\n- **C) Prioritize the canonical source rebuild + fork pin** (F3) so the fix is durable.\n- **D) Treat the #135 watcher kill-and-restart as the primary recovery**; the wrapSSE patch is defense-in-depth, not the cure.\n\n### Recommendation\nShip the patch as an interim fix (it correctly stops the permanent json hang), but: (i) do the source rebuild + fork pin immediately (F3); (ii) verify `ctl` is per-request (F4); (iii) add error surfacing on chunkTimeout abort to break the silent loop (F1/A); (iv) consider a distinct longer deadline for non-SSE to avoid F2. Do not consider #145 \"done\" until the silent retry loop is addressed separately.","created_at":"2026-08-03T21:16:33.422046065Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:pZPj4KAaCWSnW9LGqrCJ8qmum8gUo3womZ6yv6keiX4","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg4C4UrF9guXMGASYNelQp5H4XbpAMFHa4TMz8jEq3oYEAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABA63SugLntL6Euc9bDzXmVGdbhmgU1a54jomm0hriRBWAnlJ1h6NTCYBpe70NhN4VsY2TQtdkBHYtRSy2yLp+2AQ=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:pZPj4KAaCWSnW9LGqrCJ8qmum8gUo3womZ6yv6keiX4","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg4C4UrF9guXMGASYNelQp5H4XbpAMFHa4TMz8jEq3oYEAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQFtu1SxEgmxivWNib